---
import ProjectArticle from '@components/gta6-leonida-atlas/ProjectArticle.astro';
import { urls } from '@lib/urls';
---

<ProjectArticle
  path={urls.leonidaAtlasDocumentation()}
  title="Documentation"
  section="Method"
  description="How source records, confidence labels and the shared coordinate frame become the atlas you can explore."
>
  <section>
    <h2>Data model</h2>
    <p>
      Places and viewpoints are published records. A place describes a named area or feature; a
      viewpoint connects a specific piece of evidence to a place and may include an approximate map
      position. Public records retain source, authority, confidence and precision information.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Shared coordinates</h2>
    <p>
      The atlas uses the established GTADB-to-world transform: world <code>x = GTADB x × 2</code>
      and world <code>z = −GTADB y × 2</code>. The same transform drives the cartographic view and
      the 3D reconstruction so a cited point does not drift between modes.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Cartography</h2>
    <p>
      Base geography is derived from the pinned Yanis v16 GTADB raster tiles. Land, water,
      vegetation, roads and building marks are generalized into an editorial presentation. Landmark
      proximity is never used to invent road geometry. No-data areas stay restrained and unknown.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Interaction and accessibility</h2>
    <p>
      Search and evidence are optional panels over a map-first canvas. A deliberate map tap travels
      to that coordinate; panning, pinching and cancelled gestures do not. Collision-safe position
      adjustments may be applied in the 3D view and are disclosed by the interface. Keyboard-visible
      focus, labelled controls and reduced-motion preferences are part of the experience.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Availability</h2>
    <p>
      Published place and viewpoint records come from the site CMS. If that catalogue is temporarily
      unreachable, the atlas shows an unavailable state instead of generating substitute claims.
    </p>
  </section>
</ProjectArticle>
